---

Let $P(x,y)$ denote the assertion $f(f(x)+2y)=6x+f(f(y)-x)$, and let $c=f(0)$.

### Step 1: $f$ is surjective

Set $y=-\tfrac{f(x)}{2}$ in $P(x,y)$. Then $f(x)+2y=0$, so the left side becomes $f(0)=c$. The right side gives
$$c = 6x + f\!\bigl(f(-\tfrac{f(x)}{2})-x\bigr),$$
hence $f\!\bigl(f(-\tfrac{f(x)}{2})-x\bigr)=c-6x$. As $x$ ranges over $\mathbb{R}$, the value $c-6x$ ranges over all of $\mathbb{R}$, and each is attained by $f$. Therefore **$f$ is surjective**.

### Step 2: $f$ is injective

Suppose $f(a)=f(b)$. Comparing $P(a,y)$ and $P(b,y)$: since the left sides are equal,
$$6a+f(f(y)-a)=6b+f(f(y)-b)\quad\text{for all }y,$$
i.e. $f(f(y)-a)-f(f(y)-b)=6(b-a)$ for all $y$. Since $f$ is surjective, $f(y)$ ranges over all of $\mathbb{R}$; setting $t=f(y)$ and $d=b-a$, $s=t-b$:
$$\boxed{f(s+d)-f(s)=6d\quad\text{for all }s\in\mathbb{R}.}\tag{$\star$}$$

Now substitute $x\mapsto x+d$ in $P(x,y)$:
$$f(f(x+d)+2y)=6(x+d)+f(f(y)-x-d).$$

Using $(\star)$: $f(x+d)=f(x)+6d$, so the left side is $f(f(x)+2y+6d)=f(f(x)+2y)+36d$ (applying $(\star)$ six times). The right side is $6x+6d+f(f(y)-x-d)=6x+6d+f(f(y)-x)-6d=6x+f(f(y)-x)$, which equals $f(f(x)+2y)$ by the original equation. Therefore
$$f(f(x)+2y)+36d=f(f(x)+2y),$$
giving $36d=0$, hence $d=0$, i.e. $a=b$. Thus **$f$ is injective**.

### Step 3: Determine $f$

Set $x=0$ in $P(x,y)$:
$$f(c+2y)=f(f(y))\quad\text{for all }y.$$

Since $f$ is injective, $c+2y=f(y)$ for all $y$, giving
$$f(y)=2y+c.$$

### Step 4: Verification

For $f(x)=2x+c$ ($c\in\mathbb{R}$ arbitrary):
- LHS: $f(f(x)+2y)=f(2x+c+2y)=2(2x+c+2y)+c=4x+4y+3c$.
- RHS: $6x+f(f(y)-x)=6x+f(2y+c-x)=6x+2(2y+c-x)+c=6x+4y+2c-2x+c=4x+4y+3c$.

LHS $=$ RHS. ✓

### Conclusion

The solutions are exactly
$$\boxed{f(x)=2x+c,\quad c\in\mathbb{R}.}$$

